Harper Lin: opened a small PR to tighten up Troubleshooting > Error reference for 429s/timeouts/context truncation. Noticed our streaming example mixes partial-chunk parsing and status checks which has led to confusion. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/9230
Maria Gomez: thanks Harper. Can you include a tiny JS snippet too? A lot of frontend engineers copy the JS examples directly. Also please highlight Retry-After header handling for 429s.
Harper Lin: good call. I'll add a curl repro, a compact Python v2 example, and a short Node snippet showing `Retry-After` + backoff. Also planning a 2-row decision matrix (Resume vs Fallback).
Sam Patel: CI tends to flag our anchors. If you add a new section, use `#handling-429-in-streams` as the anchor so cross-links are consistent with existing docs. I'll watch the PR for markdownlint issues.
Cheng Li: for the repro, worth showing two behaviours: (A) interrupted client socket -> server later emits 429; (B) server returns 429 immediately because of rate window. The handling differs: in A you may have partial tokens but still can resume if you have a resume token; in B you should respect Retry-After and back off. Minimal curl to illustrate A:
Cheng Li: ```bash
# simulate client cut mid-stream (use a long prompt then kill the connection)
curl -N -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/responses \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $REDWOOD" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"model":"rwd-light-1","input":"<very long prompt>"}'
# kill the client after a few chunks to mimic a disconnect; server may later return 429 when processing continues
```
Lina Torres: adding a compact Node example (pseudo code) to demonstrate checking `Retry-After` and deciding to retry vs fallback:
Lina Torres: ```js
// pseudo v2 JS client
try {
  const stream = await client.responses.stream({ model: 'rwd-light-1', input: trimTail(prompt, 2048) });
  for await (const chunk of stream) handle(chunk);
} catch (err) {
  if (err.statusCode === 429) {
    const retryAfter = parseInt(err.headers?.['retry-after'] || '0', 10);
    if (retryAfter > 0) await sleep(retryAfter * 1000);
    // if operation idempotent + resume token => attempt rejoin; otherwise fallback
    decideResumeOrFallback();
  }
}
```
Harper Lin: added a short decision matrix in the PR: columns = "Action", "When safe", "Notes". Example: Resume = idempotent + resume token present; Fallback = non-idempotent or partial output acceptable (trim + retry or switch model).
Maria Gomez: also add a line under Context limits: "Clients should check response metadata for `truncated` or `partial` flags and avoid assuming the server returned the full reconstructed prompt." That helped several users avoid silent truncation issues.
Sam Patel: fixed a stale link to /sdk/v1 in another file and reran CI. PR shows one markdownlint warning (line length) — I'll tidy it.
docs-bot: PR opened: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/9230  CI queued
Cheng Li: small note for authors: when documenting resume semantics, include a short example of the resume token lifecycle (obtain token in initial response metadata, reattach on rejoin). Avoid implying resume is automatic.
Lina Torres: pushed JS snippet and a trimmed Python example demonstrating `retry_policy: { max_retries: 3, backoff: 'exponential' }`. Also added a one-sentence recommendation to prefer prefix-caching where appropriate to reduce token churn on retries.
Harper Lin: ran the curl repro in an isolated dev workspace — reproduced interrupted-stream then 429. Added explicit advice: prefer checking numeric status codes and Retry-After instead of attempting to parse incomplete chunk bodies.
Sam Patel: CI passed after the anchor and lint fixes :white_check_mark:. Docs link checks all green.
Maria Gomez: looks good. I'll add a short release-note: "Docs: clarified stream 429 handling; added Node/Python examples and decision matrix for resume vs fallback." Harper, feel free to squash-merge when you're ready.
Harper Lin: thanks — merging after squash. Closing thread.
